  "query_preview": "..\n\nShowers continue to dissipate as expected from north to south as \ndrier air filters into the low levels. This process is the result of \nsplit flow moving in aloft and 1028 mb surface high pressure \nbuilding out of central Ontario. A wind shift/cold front is being \ndriven south by the strengthening anticyclone, and will eventually \nsteer gradient flow northeasterly. Despite the influx of a drier \nCanadian air mass, stratocumulus clouds are expected to linger \novernight given the cold advection over Lake Huron. Cooler again \ntonight, with temperatures dipping into the 40s for some locations \nfarther north.\n\nReadings hold below seasonal averages through the first half of the \nweek, with mainly dry conditions prevailing as the ridge influence \ntakes command. The exception will be closer to the Ohio border where \na shortwave moves from The Plains into the Ohio Valley, before \nclosing off and stalling. Expect vicinity showers at times Monday \nthrough Tuesday, although the rest of Southeast Michigan ought to \nstay dry and breezy, particularly during the afternoon hours as the \nridge/subsidence wins out. Thermal troughing maximizes across the \nMidwest by Wednesday with 850 mb temperatures dropping into the mid \n(perhaps low) single digits (Celsius). The NAM shows that the \ncoldest air should filter across Lake Erie, setting up a north-to-\nsouth thermal gradient over southeast Lower, with portions of Metro \nDetroit possibly seeing highs capped AOB 60F.\n\nThe synoptic pattern should lock up midweek as the closed mid/upper \nlow orients in Rex Block formation, immediately south of the Ontario \nanticyclonic wave. A southern stream wave will fold into the Ohio \nValley and break the stationary waves late Wednesday into Thursday, \noffering some potential for showers, more so for the southern half \nof the forecast area. It will take some time before temperatures \nbegin to recover, as easterly lower column flow hangs on toward the \nend of the workweek.",
